This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedantam. Doing better than your parents, having a better education, living a better life, this is universally seen as a good thing. But a century ago, a poet named Christo Smirnanski wrote a parable that raises important questions about the nature of upward mobility. The tale of the stairs is about an impoverished young man standing at the foot of a marble staircase. He's gazing up at the wealthy people above him. Quarer, enjoy in themselves and having drinks while the people down below don't have enough food to eat and are really suffering.
